From: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
Subject: [PATCH] BMC reboot: fire the transition timer once, not forever

AMI's 0001-Timer-Support-for-manager-reset-operation.patch defers
RequestedBMCTransition through gTimer, an sdeventplus::utility::Timer, and
arms it with restart(interval).  restart() makes the timer PERIODIC.  With the
default timeout of 0 it fires continuously (~2x/s): every tick re-runs
executeTransition(), which re-issues StartUnit("reboot.target",
"replace-irreversibly") and logs "Setting the BMCState field to NotReady".

systemd receives the reboot transaction again and again; each re-submission
merges into the pending jobs and the queue never dispatches.  Seen on .245
2026-09-24: `systemctl is-system-running` = stopping, 179 jobs all "waiting",
none running, not a single unit stopped in 14 minutes.  SSH and HTTPS drop
every connection (systemd will not start socket-activated services while
stopping) while IPMI keeps answering, so the BMC looks half-alive until it is
AC-cycled.  Every graceful BMC reboot goes through here -- Redfish
Manager.Reset / ResetToDefaults, IPMI mc reset and factory reset -- and whether
one gets through is luck.

restartOnce() arms it as a one-shot, which is what a deferred reboot needs.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [fixes an AMI patch]
---
--- a/bmc_state_manager.cpp
+++ b/bmc_state_manager.cpp
@@ -230,7 +230,7 @@
 
     info("timeout value""{TIMEOUT}","TIMEOUT", timeOutValue);
     auto interval = std::chrono::seconds(timeOutValue); // start gTimer
-    gTimer->restart(interval);
+    gTimer->restartOnce(interval);
 
     return server::BMC::requestedBMCTransition(value);
 }
